The past two decades have witnessed the rapid development of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) as a class of highly porous crystalline materials. The tunability of both organic linkers and inorganic metal cluster based secondary building units (SBUs), combined with large surface areas and convenient chemical modification methods, have enabled the potential applications of MOFs in areas such as gas storage and separation, catalysis, chemical sensing, drug delivery, among others.2 Since many MOFs are water/moisturesensitive due to the reversible coordination bonding, considerable effort has been focused on MOFs with high thermal and chemical stability for their ultimate practical applications.3 Zirconium-based MOFs $\mathrm{^{.}z r}$ -MOFs) represent a remarkable improvement to this end:4 the high valence of $\mathrm{Zr}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ and large $\mathrm{Z\hat{r}^{\mathrm{IV}}}$ -O bond polarization can lead to a strong bonding between $\boldsymbol{Z}\mathbf{r}^{\mathrm{{IV}}}$ and carboxylate oxygens in the ligands. Since the discovery of UiO-66 in 2008, $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs with rich structural types, outstanding chemical stability, intriguing properties and functions have made significant progress.4 Nevertheless, this specific type of MOFs is still in its early stage of development. Its wider practical applications call for continuous discovery of new structures with novel topologies, which is important to understanding the fundamental correlation between framework structure and porosity, stability, and interpenetration.6
Tetrahedral ligands are inherently three-dimensional, fully extended linkers that have received considerable attention in recent years.7 However, to date there are only five $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs are composed of tetrahedral ligands.8 Four of them, including MOF- $841$ and $\mathrm{PCN}{-}521$ , have the non-interpenetrated $\mathbf{\nabla}f l u$ topology $(\mathrm{CaF}_{2}.$ , fluorite),8 which can be considered as the “pseudo” cubic close packing of the 8-connected $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters where the octahedral cavity is void space and all the tetrahedral interstitial cavities are occupied by the tetrahedral ligands (Figure 1a).8a During the synthesis of MOF- $841$ , Yaghi et al also discovered MOF-812 as a byproduct,8b which is essentially a 4,12-connected net of a rare ith topology. This topology can be viewed as the body centered packing of 12- connected $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters where half of the tetrahedral interstitial cavities are occupied by the tetrahedral ligands (Figure 1b). Although little study has been performed on MOF-812, its discovery does reveal the possibility for new topologies of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs based on other tetrahedral linkers.
Herein, we report two non-interpenetrated $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs, NPF200 and 201 (NPF: Nebraska Porous Framework), based on elongated tetrahedral linkers (Figure 1c). Both MOFs belong to the rare $_{4,12,12T_{l}}$ topology. Importantly, NPF-200 can be successfully activated and exhibits $81.6\%$ solvent accessible volume and a BET surface area of $5{,}463\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ , which is the largest among the MOFs formed from tetrahedral ligands and also the third largest among all $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs. Additionally, the isostructural NPF-201, featuring a tetravalent boroncentered linker, is an anionic $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOF that selectively uptakes $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ .
The two tetrahedral linkers $(\mathbf{L}_{4}$ and ${\bf L}_{5}^{-}$ ) were synthesized by Sonogashira coupling, followed by saponification in basic aqueous solution (see Supporting Information S-2 for detailed procedures). Colorless crystals of NPF-200 $\mathsf{[Z r_{6}(\upmu_{3}-}$ $\mathrm{O})_{4}(\upmu_{3}{-}\mathrm{OH})_{4}]_{3}[\mathrm{Zr}_{8}(\upmu_{2}{-}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{H}))_{12}](\mathrm{L}_{4})_{12}$ were obtained via a solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ with $\mathbf{L}_{4}$ in ${\cal N},{\cal N}-$ dimethylformamide (DMF) with benzoic acid as modulating agent9 at 120 $^{\circ}C$ for $4^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Single crystal X-ray diffraction reveals that NPF-200 crystalizes in the cubic space group $P m\cdot$ - $3n$ , consistent with the truncated octahedral shape of the single crystals (Figure $S_{7}\mathrm{\overline{{{\Omega}}}}$ ). The experimental powder $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of NPF-200 are in excellent agreement with the simulation (Figure 2a), demonstrating the material’s bulk purity.
NPF-200 a non-interpenetrated framework that is composed of two different types of 12-connected $\mathrm{Zr}$ clusters $(Z\mathrm{r}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{Zr_{8,}}$ ) linked by tetrahedral ligands (Figure 1c). Overall, it is a trinodal net with the $_{4,12,12T_{l}}$ topology (Schläfli symbol: $[4^{3}]^{18}[4^{4}]^{3}[4^{6}]^{3}[4^{9}]^{4})$ , which is the same with that of ${\beta\mathrm{-UH}_{3}}$ .10 It is noteworthy that NPF-200 represents the first MOF structure that exhibits this rare topology. The $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ and $\mathrm{Zr_{8}}$ clusters
in NPF-200 can be envisioned as the topological close packing phase A15 (Figure 1d),11 one of the Frank-Kasper phases used to describe the structures of intermetallics with $\mathbf{A}_{3}\mathbf{B}$ stoichiometry (e.g. $\mathrm{Nb}_{3}\mathrm{Sn})^{12}$ and certain self-assembled macromolecules.13 In a typical cubic unit cell, B atoms (as $\mathrm{Zr_{8}}$ clusters in NPF-200) occupy the body center cubic sites while a pair of A atoms (as $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters in NPF-200) occupy the face centered sites, furthering linking in infinite chains along the [100], [010], and [001] directions. Therefore, $\mathrm{Zr_{8}}$ clusters in NPF-200 lie in the center of the 12-fold distorted icosahedron (as B atoms in A15) and $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters lie in the center of 14-fold Kasper polyhedron (as A atoms in A15) (Figure 1e). Each tetrahedral ligand is connected with three $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ and one $\mathrm{Zr_{8}}$ clusters (Figure 1e), just like the H atoms in ${\upbeta\mathrm{-UH}}_{3}$ . (Figure 1d). Since A15 phase has exclusively tetrahedral interstices (see Supporting Information $S{-}7$ for detailed description), NPF200 can also be considered as part of the tetrahedral interstitial cavities are occupied by the tetrahedral ligands (Figure 1e). On average, 24 tetrahedral ligands occupy 46 interstitial tetrahedra in each unit cell, giving rise to a $52.2\%$ occupancy compared to $50\%$ in MOF-812. Further, five well-defined cages with size ranging from 12 $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ to 20 Å can be identified in NPF-200 (Figure 1f and Figure S15-19).
The $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster in NPF-200 is the commonly observed, 12- connected $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\upmu_{3}{\mathrm{-O}})_{4}(\upmu{\mathrm{-OH}})_{4}$ as in many $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs (i.e. UiO66 and its isoreticular fcu series5), which can be simplified as a cuboctahedron (Figure $\mathbf{S20}$ ). In contrast, the $\mathrm{Zr_{8}}$ cluster in NPF-200 is best described as $\mathrm{Zr_{8}(\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-O(H))_{12},}}$ with eight $\mathrm{Zr}$ atoms connecting twelve $\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-O}(\mathrm{H})$ forming a cubic $\mathrm{Zr}$ cluster in which the eight vertices are occupied by $Z\mathrm{r}$ atoms capped by twelve $\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-O}(\mathrm{H})$ occupying the edges (Figure 1c, see Supporting Information $S{-}2$ for details). The simplified $\mathrm{Zr_{8}}$ cluster in NPF-200 corresponds to a slightly distorted icosahedron (Figure S21), which nevertheless is more regular compared to that of the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters in MOF-812 (Figure S20), likely due to the coordination of four monodentate carboxylate groups in the latter. In NPF-200, each $\mathrm{Zr}$ atom in the $\mathrm{Zr_{8}}$ cluster coordinates with three $\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-O}(\mathrm{H})$ and three oxygen atoms from carboxylates, forming a distorted octahedral coordination geometry. Compared to the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster, the $\mathrm{Zr_{8}}$ cluster is much less common. Zhou et al has previously reported an example of cubic $\mathrm{Zr_{8}}$ cluster, $\mathrm{Zr_{8}(\upmu_{4}\mathrm{-O)_{6}(O H)_{8},}}$ , in PCN-221, where each edge of the $\mathrm{Zr_{8}}$ cube is bridged by a carboxylate group, and the simplified SBU corresponds to a cuboctahedron with an $\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{h}}$ symmetry (Figure S21).14 Our result represents the second example of $\mathrm{Zr_{8}}$ cluster in $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOF.
The thermal stability of NPF-200 was assessed by TGA (thermogravimetric analysis). The high decomposition temperature around $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ reveals the excellent thermal stability of the material at elevated temperature (Supporting Information, Figure $S25$ ). NPF-200 shows remarkable stability in acidic and basic conditions (Figure 2b). After soaking in aqueous solutions with $\mathrm{\pH}$ values ranging from 1 to 11 (prepared with HCl and $\mathsf{N a O H}$ respectively) for $_{24}\mathrm{~h~}$ , the PXRD patterns of NPF-200 are almost unchanged, suggesting the preserved crystallinity (Figure 2b). The $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption of NPF-200 after water treatment (Figure S11) exhibits only a minimal deviation from that of the pristine sample, indicating the framework is highly robust. However, NPF-200 does start to lose crystallinity after soaked in a strongly basic solution $\mathrm{\langlepH}=12\mathrm{\rangle}$ ) (Figure 2b).
Supercritical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ exchange15 was employed to activate NPF-200 to avoid channel collapse. With a pore volume of $\mathsf{2.17~c m^{3}~g^{-1}}$ , NPF-200 has the $\Nu_{2}$ uptake at $77^{\mathrm{K}}$ of 1403 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ the $\mathrm{\SA_{BET}}$ (Brunauer-Emmett-Teller surface area) of $5463~\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , and the $\mathrm{SA_{Langmuir}}$ (Langmuir surface area) of $68_{77}\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ Based on the calculated value by PLATON, the solvent accessible volume is $81.6\%$ .16 It is noted that NPF-200 has the largest surface area, pore size, and solvent-accessible volume among all MOFs constructed from tetrahedral ligands. The
