calculated volumetric surface area of NPF-200 is $2125~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{cm}^{-}$ 3, which is outstanding among many well-known MOFs (Table $S_{3}$ ). For certain gas uptake applications, a large volumetric surface area is crucial.17
Isoreticular expansion18 is a useful strategy to increase the pore size and surface area for $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs.19 It is however interesting to note that the ligand elongation in NPF-200 did not result in the expected $\mathbf{\nabla}f l u$ topology as in $\mathrm{PCN-}521$ . The increased rotational freedom from $\mathbf{L}_{3}$ (in $\mathrm{PCN-}521)$ to $\mathbf{L}_{4}$ (in NPF-200) likely plays a role here. In order to fulfill the coordination and topological requirement in NPF-200 (i.e. binding with one $\mathrm{Zr_{8}}$ and three $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters in $_{4,12,12T_{l}}$ topology), the tetrahedral ligand $\mathbf{L}_{4}$ needs to adopt a $C_{2\nu}$ symmetry so that the two peripheral benzoates are perpendicularly orientated to one pseudo mirror plane and two peripheral benzoates lying in the other (Figure 3a). Such conformation is made possible by the triple bond spacer between the two phenyl rings that promotes low to no torsional barrier for the vicinal phenyl rings. Indeed, other conformations of $\mathbf{L}_{4}$ are possible, as exemplified in MOF $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathbf{L}_{4}$ reported by Lin et al, where all four peripheral benzoates are perpendicularly orientated to the mirror planes (Figure 3b).7f Conversely, in $\mathsf{P C N-}52\mathbf{1}$ all four peripheral benzoates of $\mathbf{L}_{3},$ , which has the $D_{\mathrm{2d}}$ symmetry, lie in the two mirror planes to meet the requirement of coordination to four $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters in the $\mathit{f l u}$ topology (Figure $3\mathrm{c}$ ). Further, in the absence of spacer, the reduced rotation freedom between the two phenyl rings in $\mathbf{L}_{3}$ also makes it unfavorable to adopt a zero dihedral angle, which is required to adopt a similar conformation as that of $\mathbf{L}_{4}$ to form the $_{4,12,12T_{l}}$ topology.
The success in synthesizing NPF-200 offers a general way of constructing non-interpenetrated $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs with exceptional high porosity. We then next set out to synthesize an isostructure of NPF-200 using a boron-centered tetrahedral linker $\mathbf{L}_{5}$ (Figure 1d, see Supporting Information $S{-}2$ for synthetic details) that has essentially the same size and dimensionality as $\mathbf{L}_{4}$ . Since $\mathbf{L_{5}}$ is inherently negatively charged, the resulting MOF is expected to be an anionic $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOF. Both single crystal X-ray diffraction (Supporting Information, S-4) and PXRD pattern (Figure 2a) of the product from the solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ and $\mathbf{L}_{5},$ termed as NPF-201, is isostructural with NPF-200. However, activation of NPF-201 was not successful, and $\mathbf{SA}_{\mathrm{BET}}$ of NPF-201 was only $534~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ probably due to the framework collapse induced by extra framework counter cation tetrabutylammonium. Nevertheless, NPF-201 still exhibits good thermal stability (thermal decomposition temperature of $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , Figure $S26$ ) and chemical stability in solution (vide infra), which enable its potential applications in the solvated state including heterogeneous catalysis.
The anionic charge of the framework of NPF-201 was further confirmed by dye exchange experiments. As expected, NPF-201 does not adsorb the anionic dye, acid orange 7, but strongly adsorbs the cationic methylene blue (Figure 4a). The adsorbed methylene blue can be further exchanged by soaking in 0.5 M $\mathsf{N H}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}$ solution (Figure 4a). To the best of our knowledge, NPF-201 represents the first example of anionic $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOF.21 This feature opens enormous opportunities to incorporate functional cationic guests into $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs for a variety of applications.22 Here, photoactive cationic transition metal complex, $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ , was incorporated into NPF201 via ion exchange to form $\mathrm{Ru}@\mathrm{NPF}_{-201}$ (Figure 4b-c). The loading level of $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ , 0.37 Ru complex per ligand, was determined using NMR spectroscopy of an acid-digested sample (Supporting Information, Figure S8). This loading value is close to the ideal uptake (0.5 per ligand), suggesting the successful incorporation of the metal complex inside the framework. Ru@NPF-201 exhibits the characteristic emission band of $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ at $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 4d) and can be used as a heterogeneous catalyst in photo-oxidation of thioanisole. Indeed, while NPF-201 does not show any activity at all, Ru@NPF-201 gives rise to the sulfoxide product in an excellent yield $(98\%)$ , with a catalytic activity comparable to $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ indicating the reaction is not diffusion limited (Supporting Information S-9, Table S2). Importantly, ${\mathrm{Ru@NPF}}$ -201 exhibits excellent recyclability: after five times of reuse, no decrease of catalytic activity is observed (Table S2).
In summary, we have synthesized two new noninterpenetrated $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs with the rare ${\beta\mathrm{-UH}_{3}}$ -like, $_{4,12,12T_{1}}$ topology using elongated tetrahedral linkers. NPF-200 exhibits high permanent porosity $\left({\bf2.17~c m^{3}~g^{-1}}\right)$ , large surface area $(5,463\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ , and exceptional stability, which make it a promising candidate material for gas storage. Anionic NPF201 successfully incorporates photoactive $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ via cation exchange, the resulting hybrid material exhibits good photocatalytic activity and recyclability towards the photooxidation of thioanisole. Our work pointes a new direction for designing functional $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs with new topologies using elongated tetrahedral linkers.
